{"name":"Her Velour","hex":"#bb5f62","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"her","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Herero."]},{"part":"Determiner","definitions":["Belonging to her (belonging to that female person or animal, or in poetic or old-fashioned language that ship, city, season, etc).","Belonging to a person of unspecified gender ."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/her"},{"term":"velour","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A knit fabric similar to velvet, but usually somewhat coarser."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/velour"}],"entries":[{"title":"Velour","blocks":[{"text":"Velour, occasionally velours, is a plush, knitted fabric or textile similar to velvet or velveteen. It can be made from polyester, spandex, cotton, or a cotton-polyester blend. Velour is used in a wide variety of applications, including clothing and upholstery. Velour typically has a medium-length pile, shorter than velvet but longer than velveteen."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/PinkBathrobe.JPG/330px-PinkBathrobe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Velour","wikidata":"Q621044"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Velour.jpg/330px-Velour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Velour.jpg/330px-Velour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A piece of velour fabric","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVelour.jpg"}],"sounds":{"her":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2c/En-us-her.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikip